{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793734767,"nonce":"FGtmbJAzCGNzXVkBUa96HfMcRt4=","message":"Detailed startup attribution on the exact c53620ef benchmark executable (SHA-256 6a383df15c1f0ff34b22acc490fa6b9817cbc64bfa823c72ced11c110bbe688e), NUC, LLVM enabled, native snapshot active (119 functions placed), no builds, CPUs 0,2,4,6. Seven runs with TRACE_LOWER_PHASES and TRACE_LAZY plus a separate optimizer-pass trace. All checksums are result: 5. No source or checkout changes.\n\nAdditive mean breakdown of the seven diagnostic runs, measured from the launcher's CLOCK_MONOTONIC and the process's main/exit markers:\n- Lua frontend (parse, interface, scope/type inference, both typed-AST lowering passes): 2.890 ms\n- runtime construction and snapshot/plugin registration: 0.490 ms\n- compile_typed_program: 8.096 ms (roughly 5.1 ms HIR lowering/import linking and 3 ms other preparation/native placement)\n- run() outside the benchmark's timer: 11.837 ms\n- launch/loader/exit/reaping and unaccounted main-thread setup: 4.320 ms\n- measured kernel: 3.723 ms\nTotal wall: 31.356 ms. Overhead excluding the kernel: 27.633 ms. These diagnostic runs include trace overhead; keep the earlier uninstrumented medians as the performance verdict.\n\nThe dominant identified wait is first-entry compilation. TRACE_LAZY reports the caller waiting a median 11.33 ms for the bead belonging to lua$chunk. The warm-up worker's chunk compile takes a median 13.55 ms total, with 10.31 ms before code generation and 2.90 ms in Cranelift code generation. These overlap initial compilation and must not be added again to the additive breakdown. Small callee compiles generally cost 0.1-0.6 ms and overlap the kernel.\n\nThe mechanism is explicit in tiered_backend.rs: static_hot_beads identifies loops as hot before their first call; precompile_static_hot queues their compile; the interpreter tick callback's compile_first branch calls try_lazy_compile and returns native code when ready. compile_lazy_function waits on the publication condition variable if the warm-up worker already owns that bead. Thus putting the work on a worker does not hide it when entry immediately waits for publication. This is a first-entry Cranelift/body-preparation cost, not evidence of LLVM compilation taking these 11 ms.\n\nA separate TRACE_OPT_PHASES run shows four optimizer rounds on lua$chunk, including inlining/error-flag work, repeated CSE, allocation scalarization and loop passes. The body-preparation timing also includes other pre-codegen work, so do not attribute all 10 ms to one pass. The next high-impact investigation is this blocking first-entry policy and its body preparation, with interpreter-first/OSR behavior and end-to-end performance validated. Native library snapshot loading is already functioning.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-startup-breakdown/{nuc-traces.json,breakdown.json,trace-7.log}.","files":null}]}